1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,040
 Hey, everybody, Ryan here and welcome back to our Patient Management series.

2
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:09,600
 We're going to cover a lot of material in this video, and we're going to be talking

3
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:12,380
 about the evaluation of dental literature.

4
00:00:12,380 --> 00:00:15,780
 It's really easy to get bogged down by this topic.

5
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:19,760
 Evidence-based dentistry can be really dry, but I'm going to hit the highlights for you.

6
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:24,400
 No nonsense, high-yield facts, let's go.

7
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:30,760
 So medicine and dentistry in particular in the 1990s underwent a paradigm shift.

8
00:00:30,760 --> 00:00:37,480
 We used to value the opinions of experts like GV Black above all else, but now we value

9
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:40,600
 approaches based on evidence.

10
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:48,400
 Is there proof that certain materials and certain techniques actually work in a majority of patients?

11
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:55,720
 The quality of evidence is rated from best to worst in a so-called hierarchy of evidence.

12
00:00:55,720 --> 00:01:01,080
 At the top of the pyramid is the meta-analysis and systematic reviews.

13
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:06,960
 And then going from best to worst, we have randomized clinical trials, cohort studies,

14
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:13,040
 case control studies, cross-sectional studies or surveys, case reports, which are reports

15
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:19,840
 of unique patients or findings, then we have our expert opinions like GV Black, and finally,

16
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:27,080
 at the bottom, animal studies and test tube lab bench in vitro studies.

17
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:33,040
 And these two are not nearly as clinically relevant as the other, since why they're italicized,

18
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:35,760
 because you're not using a human model.

19
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:38,200
 So they're at the bottom of the hierarchy.

20
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:45,760
 Now we're going to cover a lot, or most of these in the next couple slides.

21
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:53,440
 So our first big category of dental literature are the descriptive or epidemiological studies.

22
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:58,800
 So epidemiology is the study of the distribution and determinants of disease.

23
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:04,640
 And we had a whole video dedicated to this topic, so you're already experts on this.

24
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:10,520
 And so these studies are large-scale community-based studies to quantify the disease status in

25
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:11,960
 a community.

26
00:02:11,960 --> 00:02:17,160
 How many people in this population have caries, have gum disease, et cetera?

27
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:22,240
 And so we're using these measuring tools that we talked about in the epidemiology video,

28
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:25,520
 like DMFT, gingival index.

29
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:32,000
 Think of easy measuring tools that clinicians can employ to gather lots of data on a huge

30
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:37,960
 entire communities, and then from that determine the prevalence of, again, tooth decay, gum

31
00:02:37,960 --> 00:02:43,440
 disease, which is the proportion of a given population that's affected by that condition

32
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:47,760
 at a given time.

33
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:55,160
 Our next big picture group of dental literature are the analytical or observational studies.

34
00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:58,480
 This is really the meat and potatoes of this video.

35
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:06,000
 And so these studies are designed to determine the etiology or cause of a disease.

36
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,920
 And note that all of these four study designs here determine association, not causation.

37
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:20,640
 So they may or may not represent causal relationships between a factor or risk factor in a condition,

38
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:21,800
 et cetera.

39
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:26,040
 So the first one we're going to talk about here is the cross-sectional study.

40
00:03:26,040 --> 00:03:32,200
 And this one's very similar to what we just talked about, but on a smaller scale than

41
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:35,560
 the community-based epidemiologic studies.

42
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:41,440
 So cross-sectional studies involve a survey or measurements that are taken to represent

43
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:43,960
 a snapshot in time.

44
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:47,080
 And so this is going to measure prevalence.

45
00:03:47,080 --> 00:03:53,800
 For example, we could examine a group of people who drink alcohol and see how many of those

46
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:57,240
 people have oral cancer and how many do not.

47
00:03:57,240 --> 00:04:02,840
 So literally, you could just sample a classroom or patients at a clinic, and this all can

48
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:04,520
 be done in the same day.

49
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:10,320
 There's no need for any follow-up after you gather your data.

50
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:18,800
 Now everything beyond the cross-sectional study in this slide is what's called a longitudinal

51
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:19,800
 study.

52
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:25,520
 So these three are longitudinal because it requires you to follow people over a certain

53
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:27,120
 period of time.

54
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:33,440
 Cross-sectional like the descriptive epidemiologic studies can be done at one point in time, just

55
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:36,800
 gathering whatever present data we have at that day.

56
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:42,120
 But these three we have to follow patients or have to have data from patients over at

57
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:44,560
 least a couple years.

58
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:51,680
 So the case control study involves people with a condition, which are called the cases,

59
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:55,880
 and they're compared to people without that condition, which are called the controls,

60
00:04:55,880 --> 00:04:59,560
 hence the name case control.

61
00:04:59,560 --> 00:05:02,720
 Now this is done in the past.

62
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:08,760
 So this is a retrospective study design where the follow-up period I just talked about has

63
00:05:08,760 --> 00:05:11,600
 actually already been completed.

64
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:18,640
 So for example, you'd have separate groups of people and those with oral cancer and

65
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:23,840
 those without it, and then you'd search through their past medical records to look for the

66
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:27,520
 presence or absence of alcohol drinking.

67
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:30,320
 That's our risk factor for these examples.

68
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:34,800
 And you do this in order to determine the odds ratio.

69
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:40,240
 How likely is it that alcohol is associated with oral cancer?

70
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:46,320
 So this is a retrospective study because we're looking in the past, but it did involve some

71
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:48,760
 period where the patients had to be followed.

72
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:54,800
 So it's still, in this case, it's still a longitudinal study design.

73
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:58,040
 Next we have the prospective cohort study.

74
00:05:58,040 --> 00:06:01,880
 So these next two are both cohort studies.

75
00:06:01,880 --> 00:06:10,520
 So cohort is a group of subjects followed for a long time to see if they develop some condition.

76
00:06:10,520 --> 00:06:16,520
 So prospective cohort study means that the cohort was followed through time to see who

77
00:06:16,520 --> 00:06:20,040
 develops a certain disease.

78
00:06:20,040 --> 00:06:25,760
 So let's say we track a group of people for five years to see how many of them develop

79
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:27,400
 oral cancer.

80
00:06:27,400 --> 00:06:33,480
 And then we would compare the incidence of oral cancer in people who drink alcohol and

81
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:35,640
 those who do not.

82
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:40,800
 And incidence is the number of new cases that appear over time.

83
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:46,200
 And then from that information you could determine the relative risk, which is probability of

84
00:06:46,200 --> 00:06:52,320
 drinkers getting oral cancer versus non-drinkers getting oral cancer.

85
00:06:52,320 --> 00:06:58,520
 And lastly we have the retrospective cohort study where we would look back after following

86
00:06:58,520 --> 00:07:04,560
 this cohort and decide, well, you know what, maybe we want to see who developed carries.

87
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:10,200
 And so then we could compare the incidence of carries between the drinkers and the non-drinkers.

88
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:15,040
 So it's retrospective because patients were already followed over time and the data is

89
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:16,320
 already there.

90
00:07:16,320 --> 00:07:21,120
 Now we just need to compile and analyze that data.

91
00:07:21,120 --> 00:07:27,540
 So some people ask, well, what's the difference between retrospective cohort study and case

92
00:07:27,540 --> 00:07:29,080
 control study?

93
00:07:29,080 --> 00:07:32,920
 Because they're both retrospective, they're both looking in the past.

94
00:07:32,920 --> 00:07:37,040
 And I don't really understand what's the difference between the two.

95
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:43,640
 So the retrospective cohort study tries to determine the risk of developing a disease

96
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:46,320
 with already known exposure factors.

97
00:07:46,320 --> 00:07:51,600
 So say we already know that alcohol contributes to oral cancer, that smoking is linked to

98
00:07:51,600 --> 00:07:53,600
 peridontal disease.

99
00:07:53,600 --> 00:07:59,480
 Whereas the case control study will try to determine the possible exposure factors after

100
00:07:59,480 --> 00:08:02,160
 a known disease incidence.

101
00:08:02,160 --> 00:08:07,800
 So they're essentially flip-flops from one another, one's trying to gather information

102
00:08:07,800 --> 00:08:12,560
 about risk of developing a disease and we already know the exposure factors.

103
00:08:12,560 --> 00:08:17,520
 The other one's trying to figure out what possible exposure factors there are.

104
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:20,200
 So it's a really important distinction to make.

105
00:08:20,200 --> 00:08:26,040
 And hopefully that can help simplify those four seemingly complex, but when you break

106
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:30,480
 it down, not too bad, analytical study designs.

107
00:08:30,480 --> 00:08:35,400
 And for the board exam, I bolded prevalence, odds ratio, and then incidence and relative

108
00:08:35,400 --> 00:08:38,000
 risk because they like to ask about that.

109
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:43,880
 So if you can simply remember which of the study designs are trying to gather which data

110
00:08:43,880 --> 00:08:48,440
 that's really helpful for the exam.

111
00:08:48,440 --> 00:08:51,520
 And lastly we have the experimental studies.

112
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:57,200
 And these ones try to determine the effectiveness of a given therapy.

113
00:08:57,200 --> 00:09:00,600
 So think a medication or drug, et cetera.

114
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:07,440
 So the randomized clinical trial aims to isolate one factor, say a new drug, and examine its

115
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:15,120
 contribution to a patient's health by holding all other factors as constant as possible.

116
00:09:15,120 --> 00:09:16,960
 And that's really hard to do.

117
00:09:16,960 --> 00:09:20,360
 But the best tool is randomization.

118
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:28,400
 So you would get a probability sample where you randomly select people from a list or database

119
00:09:28,400 --> 00:09:35,240
 and then randomly allocate them, randomly allocate those individuals into different treatment

120
00:09:35,240 --> 00:09:40,560
 groups using a random number generator or some equivalent.

121
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:45,480
 And so randomization, both of the selection of the people you're using in your study and

122
00:09:45,480 --> 00:09:51,040
 then how they're allocated into different like placebo and experimental treatment groups,

123
00:09:51,040 --> 00:09:58,400
 randomization minimizes selection bias and is the best control for confounding variables,

124
00:09:58,400 --> 00:10:02,040
 which we'll talk about a little bit later in the video.

125
00:10:02,040 --> 00:10:09,200
 And blinding is another great method for creating an ideal clinical trial.

126
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:17,040
 Blinding means concealing allocation information to minimize the biased assessment of outcomes.

127
00:10:17,040 --> 00:10:23,680
 So a single blind study means that the participants or the subjects are blind to what group they

128
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:24,680
 they're in.

129
00:10:24,680 --> 00:10:27,000
 They don't know if they're taking a placebo or they don't know if they're taking this

130
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:28,640
 new drug.

131
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:34,360
 Multiple blind studies are even better because both the participants and the experimenters

132
00:10:34,360 --> 00:10:39,920
 or the investigators are blind and they don't know what they don't know the subjects don't

133
00:10:39,920 --> 00:10:45,280
 know what drug they're taking and the investigators don't know what data they're collecting for

134
00:10:45,280 --> 00:10:46,280
 which group.

135
00:10:46,280 --> 00:10:52,160
 So that's the best way to blind a study.

136
00:10:52,160 --> 00:10:57,440
 Alright so let's get into more of the nitty-gritty with the biostatistics.

137
00:10:57,440 --> 00:11:01,000
 And so some of this stuff is definitely tested on the board exam and I'm going to try to

138
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:07,040
 hit the highlights so we don't get to, again, to bog down into the nitty-gritty details.

139
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:11,840
 So frequency distributions refer to these curves down here.

140
00:11:11,840 --> 00:11:18,080
 We have a normal distribution in the middle, which is this classic bell-shaped curve.

141
00:11:18,080 --> 00:11:21,040
 In the middle is the mean, median, and mode.

142
00:11:21,040 --> 00:11:25,760
 We'll talk about the measures of central tendency in the next slide.

143
00:11:25,760 --> 00:11:31,440
 But if you have any extra room for memorization, you can remember that within one standard

144
00:11:31,440 --> 00:11:36,960
 deviation from the center of this curve, you have 68% of subjects.

145
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:41,640
 If we go two standard deviations away, we have 95%.

146
00:11:41,640 --> 00:11:47,360
 If we go three standard deviations away, we have 99.7% of the subjects.

147
00:11:47,360 --> 00:11:52,360
 And so we don't have to, I don't think you necessarily have to memorize these numbers

148
00:11:52,360 --> 00:11:56,560
 again, and if you don't understand standard deviation right now, don't worry about it,

149
00:11:56,560 --> 00:11:59,520
 we're going to be talking about it in just a little bit.

150
00:11:59,520 --> 00:12:04,560
 We can also have a skewed distribution, which is what these two are showing, positively

151
00:12:04,560 --> 00:12:10,680
 skewed if the tail, so this part is the tail goes to the right, negatively skewed if the

152
00:12:10,680 --> 00:12:13,800
 tail goes off to the left.

153
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:20,040
 Bi-modal distribution just means instead of one peak, you have two peaks in your data

154
00:12:20,040 --> 00:12:23,760
 collection.

155
00:12:23,760 --> 00:12:25,760
 Alright measures of central tendency.

156
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:28,280
 This I have seen tested on quite a bit.

157
00:12:28,280 --> 00:12:34,040
 Central tendency means how to, it allows you to characterize a data set by some central

158
00:12:34,040 --> 00:12:35,440
 numbers.

159
00:12:35,440 --> 00:12:39,320
 So I'm sure you've all heard of these things at some point in math class.

160
00:12:39,320 --> 00:12:43,600
 We have mean, which is the average value of a data set.

161
00:12:43,600 --> 00:12:48,680
 So for the mean, you would add up, let's say, let's just have this example data set.

162
00:12:48,680 --> 00:12:55,200
 You'd add up all of these values, and then divide by the number or the amount of values

163
00:12:55,200 --> 00:12:57,160
 in the data set.

164
00:12:57,160 --> 00:13:03,200
 So for example, the sum of all these numbers is 95, and there are 11 of them.

165
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:08,840
 So we would take 95, divide it by 11, and we get 8.63.

166
00:13:08,840 --> 00:13:12,360
 And so that would be the mean for this data set.

167
00:13:12,360 --> 00:13:15,680
 The median is the middle value.

168
00:13:15,680 --> 00:13:24,960
 So for this one, we simply cross out the outside numbers until we get to the middle number of

169
00:13:24,960 --> 00:13:28,280
 the data set.

170
00:13:28,280 --> 00:13:29,280
 So we're almost there.

171
00:13:29,280 --> 00:13:35,680
 We go 5 from either side, and 6 would be our median for this data set.

172
00:13:35,680 --> 00:13:40,080
 Now there's an even number of values, you'd have two numbers at the middle, and you simply

173
00:13:40,080 --> 00:13:45,320
 would take an average or mean of those two values.

174
00:13:45,320 --> 00:13:49,680
 And lastly, the mode is the most frequent measurement in a set of data.

175
00:13:49,680 --> 00:13:53,840
 So for these numbers, unfortunately I've crossed them all out, but you can see that

176
00:13:53,840 --> 00:13:57,240
 4 is the one that occurs most frequently.

177
00:13:57,240 --> 00:14:00,560
 So 4 would be the mode of this data set.

178
00:14:00,560 --> 00:14:05,080
 And believe it or not, these are things I would definitely know how to do on a data set for

179
00:14:05,080 --> 00:14:06,080
 the board exam.

180
00:14:06,080 --> 00:14:13,120
 You could get like test scores or some epidemiologic measure and have to figure out the mean, median

181
00:14:13,120 --> 00:14:14,840
 or mode of those numbers.

182
00:14:14,840 --> 00:14:19,880
 So luckily, it's nice and easy once you know how to do it.

183
00:14:19,880 --> 00:14:22,960
 And then we also have measures of dispersion.

184
00:14:22,960 --> 00:14:29,760
 So dispersion, referring to how spread out the values are from each other.

185
00:14:29,760 --> 00:14:34,960
 So the range is the maximum value minus the minimum value.

186
00:14:34,960 --> 00:14:40,720
 So for our same data set, we would take the max, which is 21, and subtract 2.

187
00:14:40,720 --> 00:14:45,640
 And so the range for this data set would be 19.

188
00:14:45,640 --> 00:14:52,000
 Variance is how spread out the individual values are from the mean.

189
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,840
 So this one's actually a bit tricky to calculate.

190
00:14:54,840 --> 00:14:57,880
 You never would have to do this for the board exam.

191
00:14:57,880 --> 00:15:05,360
 But what you would do is figure out the mean, which we got as 8.63, and then for each number,

192
00:15:05,360 --> 00:15:12,040
 so each of these 11 numbers, you would subtract the mean from that number, and then square

193
00:15:12,040 --> 00:15:13,840
 the result.

194
00:15:13,840 --> 00:15:16,320
 So this would give you the squared difference.

195
00:15:16,320 --> 00:15:20,720
 And then you'd calculate the average of those squared differences.

196
00:15:20,720 --> 00:15:22,840
 So don't worry about that calculation.

197
00:15:22,840 --> 00:15:29,640
 But for this data set, if you're curious, the variance is 42.049.

198
00:15:29,640 --> 00:15:35,520
 Standard deviation is just the square root of that, so it would be 6.48.

199
00:15:35,520 --> 00:15:42,080
 So the larger, and the important part here is the larger that this number is, the more

200
00:15:42,080 --> 00:15:46,720
 spread out the values are from one another.

201
00:15:46,720 --> 00:15:51,720
 So if we had a standard deviation of 1, all these numbers would be a lot closer.

202
00:15:51,720 --> 00:15:56,640
 Since we're a little bit higher at 6.48, that's explaining how we have 2, and then we have

203
00:15:56,640 --> 00:15:58,000
 all the way up to 21.

204
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:02,640
 There's a bit more of a spread between these numbers.

205
00:16:02,640 --> 00:16:08,960
 So where this all becomes especially important is considering outliers.

206
00:16:08,960 --> 00:16:15,280
 So this is a histogram, which is used to represent variance, and in this case, it's a representation

207
00:16:15,280 --> 00:16:21,840
 of that bell curve that we looked at with a bit of a positively skewed bell curve.

208
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:27,960
 And it's essentially in vertical bar format, which is how histograms are made.

209
00:16:27,960 --> 00:16:33,840
 You'll notice that these values follow a somewhat normal distribution, but we have one outlier

210
00:16:33,840 --> 00:16:36,480
 way over here.

211
00:16:36,480 --> 00:16:42,240
 And so think for a second, is an outlier like this going to have more impact on central

212
00:16:42,240 --> 00:16:49,800
 tendency or the measures of dispersion.

213
00:16:49,800 --> 00:16:56,000
 So will this outlier have a greater impact on central tendency or dispersion?

214
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:00,120
 So it's going to affect dispersion a lot more.

215
00:17:00,120 --> 00:17:06,560
 The range, variance, and standard deviation are all way more impacted by this value, which

216
00:17:06,560 --> 00:17:10,080
 is going to increase all of those values significantly.

217
00:17:10,080 --> 00:17:16,800
 It's going to have almost no effect on mode or median, and maybe some nominal effect on

218
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:22,420
 the mean, but not a whole lot because there are a bunch of other data points here.

219
00:17:22,420 --> 00:17:26,920
 But you can imagine that the range, whatever these numbers aren't listed, but whatever

220
00:17:26,920 --> 00:17:31,920
 this, subtracted by this, is going to be a lot different than this number subtracted

221
00:17:31,920 --> 00:17:33,560
 from this number.

222
00:17:33,560 --> 00:17:40,800
 All right, now we can talk about quality of a diagnostic test.

223
00:17:40,800 --> 00:17:44,920
 So we have reliability and validity.

224
00:17:44,920 --> 00:17:51,360
 So reliability is all about, are you getting consistent results from that test?

225
00:17:51,360 --> 00:17:56,720
 You have multiple operators using it, using this diagnostic tool, multiple studies that

226
00:17:56,720 --> 00:17:57,980
 have looked at it.

227
00:17:57,980 --> 00:18:01,320
 How much do they all agree with each other?

228
00:18:01,320 --> 00:18:03,240
 And I love this analogy right here.

229
00:18:03,240 --> 00:18:06,040
 It's like shooting arrows at a target.

230
00:18:06,040 --> 00:18:13,000
 And reliability means that your methods are reliably producing precise results that are

231
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,760
 all close to one another.

232
00:18:15,760 --> 00:18:21,920
 So it doesn't necessarily mean like they're accurate, they're close to the bullseye, but

233
00:18:21,920 --> 00:18:25,160
 all these arrows are grouped together quite nicely.

234
00:18:25,160 --> 00:18:27,800
 So that's an example of reliability.

235
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:32,160
 Whereas validity is looking at are they accurate results?

236
00:18:32,160 --> 00:18:37,560
 How close to the truth or to the bullseye are those results?

237
00:18:37,560 --> 00:18:44,920
 So say for a new diagnostic tool, like diagneton came out a few years ago, which produces fluorescence

238
00:18:44,920 --> 00:18:52,360
 for cavity detection, how accurate is it to distinguish between actual carries and no

239
00:18:52,360 --> 00:18:53,360
 carries?

240
00:18:53,360 --> 00:19:01,520
 Of course, we'd ideally want a test that is both reliable or precise and valid or accurate.

241
00:19:01,520 --> 00:19:08,240
 So we'd want a close grouping of arrows right around the bullseye.

242
00:19:08,240 --> 00:19:13,560
 Other things to consider are sensitivity and specificity.

243
00:19:13,560 --> 00:19:18,640
 Sensitivity means that you're correctly or the test is correctly identifying people who

244
00:19:18,640 --> 00:19:20,840
 have disease.

245
00:19:20,840 --> 00:19:25,360
 Specificity means that the diagnostic test is correctly identifying people who do not

246
00:19:25,360 --> 00:19:28,800
 have the disease or are healthy.

247
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:35,880
 So how I remember this is that the S and the E are next to each other in both sensitivity

248
00:19:35,880 --> 00:19:39,080
 and disease, twice in fact.

249
00:19:39,080 --> 00:19:44,080
 So that's a great way to remember between sensitivity and specificity, which otherwise

250
00:19:44,080 --> 00:19:46,640
 sound very similar.

251
00:19:46,640 --> 00:19:50,720
 So this table down here is incredibly important to understand.

252
00:19:50,720 --> 00:19:57,000
 So let's take a moment to go through what all these acronyms mean.

253
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:05,920
 The top row is what they actually have as determined by some gold standard measurement.

254
00:20:05,920 --> 00:20:12,720
 So let's say for hypothetical purposes, this is a crystal ball that tells us the absolute

255
00:20:12,720 --> 00:20:19,720
 truth for a subject, for a patient, if they have carries or if they don't have carries.

256
00:20:19,720 --> 00:20:25,240
 The left column is what our experimental method says they have.

257
00:20:25,240 --> 00:20:31,840
 So say we're looking at diagonal then, how valid, how reliable is it, and we're going

258
00:20:31,840 --> 00:20:39,280
 to look at the sensitivity and specificity of this new experimental method.

259
00:20:39,280 --> 00:20:43,800
 So first we have TP, this is true positive.

260
00:20:43,800 --> 00:20:50,440
 So this means that the patient actually has carries and our diagnostic tool or test says

261
00:20:50,440 --> 00:20:51,600
 they have carries.

262
00:20:51,600 --> 00:20:57,160
 So this is a success, it was correct and diagnosing.

263
00:20:57,160 --> 00:21:01,320
 Down here, FN stands for false negative.

264
00:21:01,320 --> 00:21:06,840
 So our test says they don't have carries when they actually do.

265
00:21:06,840 --> 00:21:11,520
 So we're erroneously excluding them from the carries group.

266
00:21:11,520 --> 00:21:15,160
 So false negative would be a failure of the test.

267
00:21:15,160 --> 00:21:17,520
 Now let's move over to this column.

268
00:21:17,520 --> 00:21:23,440
 CTN stands for true negative, so this means that the patient doesn't have carries and

269
00:21:23,440 --> 00:21:28,400
 our diagnostic tool successfully diagnose them as not having carries.

270
00:21:28,400 --> 00:21:31,480
 So that's another means of success.

271
00:21:31,480 --> 00:21:35,880
 And lastly we have FP, which you can guess is false positive.

272
00:21:35,880 --> 00:21:40,320
 And this is where our test says they have carries when they actually don't.

273
00:21:40,320 --> 00:21:45,080
 So we erroneously assign them to the carries group.

274
00:21:45,080 --> 00:21:53,760
 So again our test succeeds when we have TP or TN and it fails when we have FN or FP.

275
00:21:53,760 --> 00:22:05,440
 So a high amount of true positive relative to false negative is when we have high sensitivity.

276
00:22:05,440 --> 00:22:14,560
 And a high amount of true negative relative to false positive is when we have high specificity.

277
00:22:14,560 --> 00:22:22,360
 And so a perfect sensitivity and specificity would be 1.0 for each of them.

278
00:22:22,360 --> 00:22:28,800
 That would be having a perfect only true positive and only true negatives and we didn't get

279
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:32,720
 any false negatives or false positives at all.

280
00:22:32,720 --> 00:22:38,400
 But if we, let's say we got 0.5 for each of these, that's about as low as we could get

281
00:22:38,400 --> 00:22:43,480
 because then essentially we're just flipping a coin to see if they have carries or not.

282
00:22:43,480 --> 00:22:47,480
 So again, if we're looking at this column, we're in the carries column.

283
00:22:47,480 --> 00:22:55,080
 So this is sensitivity and then this column, no carries, we're looking for the accuracy

284
00:22:55,080 --> 00:22:57,960
 of detecting health.

285
00:22:57,960 --> 00:23:03,960
 And so this is a great tool if you can just remember how to draw out this chart and write

286
00:23:03,960 --> 00:23:09,680
 in these acronyms, it'll be really valuable for test day if you get any kinds of questions

287
00:23:09,680 --> 00:23:15,360
 about sensitivity and specificity which I guarantee will come up to some degree.

288
00:23:15,360 --> 00:23:22,720
 All right, next, let's go into inferential statistics.

289
00:23:22,720 --> 00:23:28,760
 So statistical significance is represented by the P value, which I'm sure you've seen

290
00:23:28,760 --> 00:23:32,440
 in most every journal article you've read.

291
00:23:32,440 --> 00:23:37,360
 And so probability that two variables are unrelated.

292
00:23:37,360 --> 00:23:42,000
 And we usually set this value, this P value as 0.05.

293
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:49,080
 And if P is less than 0.05, then we reject the null hypothesis, which I'll explain in

294
00:23:49,080 --> 00:23:51,320
 the next slide.

295
00:23:51,320 --> 00:23:55,840
 And say there's some connection between two variables.

296
00:23:55,840 --> 00:23:59,920
 Again, let's go back to our previous example, alcohol and oral cancer.

297
00:23:59,920 --> 00:24:05,360
 So for less than 0.05, we would accept that there's some connection between the two.

298
00:24:05,360 --> 00:24:10,040
 But if P is greater than 0.05, we accept the null hypothesis.

299
00:24:10,040 --> 00:24:16,800
 And any interesting connection between alcohol and oral cancer is just coincidental and due

300
00:24:16,800 --> 00:24:20,000
 to chance.

301
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:27,080
 So the null hypothesis is a hypothesis which the researcher tries to disprove, reject,

302
00:24:27,080 --> 00:24:28,080
 or nullify.

303
00:24:28,080 --> 00:24:35,240
 Now an example, null hypothesis is there is no difference between carries prevalence in

304
00:24:35,240 --> 00:24:40,160
 a fluoridated population and non-fluoridated population.

305
00:24:40,160 --> 00:24:44,920
 And people have conducted research to disprove this exact statement.

306
00:24:44,920 --> 00:24:50,120
 And in doing so, prove that fluoride helps prevent carries.

307
00:24:50,120 --> 00:24:56,120
 So let's return to our table from before, because good news, it applies here too.

308
00:24:56,120 --> 00:25:00,700
 The same as before, our top row is our crystal ball.

309
00:25:00,700 --> 00:25:07,240
 So the first column means that our null hypothesis is actually false.

310
00:25:07,240 --> 00:25:10,480
 So fluoride actually helps prevent carries.

311
00:25:10,480 --> 00:25:15,440
 And the second column is if our null hypothesis turns out to be true.

312
00:25:15,440 --> 00:25:19,560
 So it turns out fluoride doesn't help at all.

313
00:25:19,560 --> 00:25:24,960
 And the left side of our table is once again, our research results.

314
00:25:24,960 --> 00:25:26,980
 But our data shows.

315
00:25:26,980 --> 00:25:33,200
 So does diagonal work to find carries, does fluoride work to prevent carries, etc.

316
00:25:33,200 --> 00:25:39,880
 So let's start with the first column, where the null hypothesis is false.

317
00:25:39,880 --> 00:25:45,320
 So after running our statistics, our p-value is less than 0.05.

318
00:25:45,320 --> 00:25:50,160
 So our research tells us we have to reject the null hypothesis.

319
00:25:50,160 --> 00:25:52,280
 So our research worked.

320
00:25:52,280 --> 00:25:56,640
 Now let's say our p-value was greater than 0.05.

321
00:25:56,640 --> 00:26:01,220
 And we would have to accept the null hypothesis.

322
00:26:01,220 --> 00:26:05,480
 And for this first column, this would be incorrect.

323
00:26:05,480 --> 00:26:14,560
 So this is known as type 2 or beta error, where we accepted a null hypothesis that should

324
00:26:14,560 --> 00:26:18,600
 have been rejected.

325
00:26:18,600 --> 00:26:23,320
 And it's because our study didn't have enough power.

326
00:26:23,320 --> 00:26:26,000
 Now let's look at the second column.

327
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,880
 Say the null hypothesis is true.

328
00:26:28,880 --> 00:26:36,480
 And that's what our study said, said to accept the null hypothesis.

329
00:26:36,480 --> 00:26:41,380
 And so that would be a correct research result.

330
00:26:41,380 --> 00:26:46,880
 Not necessarily a fulfilling one, but it would be correct nonetheless.

331
00:26:46,880 --> 00:26:53,120
 But if our research told us to reject the null hypothesis, when it was actually true,

332
00:26:53,120 --> 00:26:57,600
 that is known as type 1 or alpha error.

333
00:26:57,600 --> 00:27:01,840
 We rejected a null hypothesis that should have been accepted.

334
00:27:01,840 --> 00:27:09,080
 That's because our p-value was at fault.

335
00:27:09,080 --> 00:27:12,120
 Alright next let's talk about correlation analysis.

336
00:27:12,120 --> 00:27:18,400
 So correlation, the correlation coefficient, or r, is a statistical measure that represents

337
00:27:18,400 --> 00:27:25,080
 the strength of the relationship between two quantitative variables.

338
00:27:25,080 --> 00:27:30,160
 And we'll talk more about quantitative versus qualitative a bit later.

339
00:27:30,160 --> 00:27:36,200
 So this r-value is always between negative 1 and positive 1.

340
00:27:36,200 --> 00:27:42,240
 You can't have anything like positive 2 or positive anything above 1, anything below

341
00:27:42,240 --> 00:27:44,160
 negative 1.

342
00:27:44,160 --> 00:27:50,640
 Positive 1 means that all the values lie perfectly on a positively sloping line.

343
00:27:50,640 --> 00:27:55,600
 It's very unlikely that you get all the results on a perfect, that you could graph them on

344
00:27:55,600 --> 00:28:01,480
 a perfect line, but that's the one end of the spectrum.

345
00:28:01,480 --> 00:28:07,000
 The other end of the spectrum, negative 1, means that they lie on a perfectly negative

346
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:09,000
 sloping line.

347
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:14,640
 Now it doesn't matter what the exact slope is, just how close the points are to that line

348
00:28:14,640 --> 00:28:17,200
 of best fit.

349
00:28:17,200 --> 00:28:23,320
 Now as you get closer to zero, we go from 1 to 0.7, they're a little bit more spread

350
00:28:23,320 --> 00:28:31,600
 out, 0.3, they're really all over the place, but they still kind of match this line of best

351
00:28:31,600 --> 00:28:37,280
 fit with a positive slope, and if you get to zero, the middle between negative 1 and

352
00:28:37,280 --> 00:28:43,040
 point positive 1, that means there's no linear relationship whatsoever.

353
00:28:43,040 --> 00:28:47,320
 So as you get closer to zero, the data points get more spread out from the line when you

354
00:28:47,320 --> 00:28:54,040
 get to zero, that means there's no linear relationship between the data coordinates.

355
00:28:54,040 --> 00:28:59,800
 You can also do a multiple regression, and the plot looks similar to these, and a multiple

356
00:28:59,800 --> 00:29:05,840
 regression provides a mathematical model for predicting relationships between a dependent

357
00:29:05,840 --> 00:29:09,840
 variable and one or more independent variables.

358
00:29:09,840 --> 00:29:16,080
 So it's a more complex version of this, but don't worry too much about that.

359
00:29:16,080 --> 00:29:21,280
 So more inferential statistics, these are all statistical analyses that are used for

360
00:29:21,280 --> 00:29:24,400
 testing a hypothesis.

361
00:29:24,400 --> 00:29:30,600
 The chi-square test, and this is the Greek letter chi, measures the association between

362
00:29:30,600 --> 00:29:34,120
 two categorical values.

363
00:29:34,120 --> 00:29:39,740
 So let's say we ask a group of people, what do you prefer, cats or dogs?

364
00:29:39,740 --> 00:29:43,120
 And let's say the group consists of both men and women.

365
00:29:43,120 --> 00:29:50,480
 So the chi-square test could tell you if gender and preference for cats or dogs are related

366
00:29:50,480 --> 00:29:51,840
 to one another.

367
00:29:51,840 --> 00:29:57,440
 So we would make a two by two table, men and women on the left and cats and dogs on the

368
00:29:57,440 --> 00:30:00,960
 top, and then we would run a chi-square test.

369
00:30:00,960 --> 00:30:06,320
 And this will, again, like most tests, give you a p-value.

370
00:30:06,320 --> 00:30:11,520
 And so this is where things kind of work itself out, and there's a lot of overlap between

371
00:30:11,520 --> 00:30:17,860
 these concepts, because if our p-value here is less than 0.05, we would reject the null

372
00:30:17,860 --> 00:30:24,560
 hypothesis, and there would be a statistically significant difference between the gender and

373
00:30:24,560 --> 00:30:26,760
 preference for cats or dogs.

374
00:30:26,760 --> 00:30:31,800
 If p is greater than 0.05, we would accept the null hypothesis, and we could say that

375
00:30:31,800 --> 00:30:37,200
 gender is not significant in determining preference for cats or dogs.

376
00:30:37,200 --> 00:30:42,480
 So chi-squared comes down to comparing two groups and see how similar or different they

377
00:30:42,480 --> 00:30:44,280
 are.

378
00:30:44,280 --> 00:30:50,720
 The t-test is another really popular one, and this is used to compare whether the means

379
00:30:50,720 --> 00:30:54,360
 of two groups are statistically different.

380
00:30:54,360 --> 00:30:59,440
 So say we have a placebo group and an experimental drug group.

381
00:30:59,440 --> 00:31:04,000
 The null hypothesis is that there's no difference between them.

382
00:31:04,000 --> 00:31:09,280
 And we're hoping there is a difference, and it would mean that the new drug is efficacious.

383
00:31:09,280 --> 00:31:16,040
 So t-test will help us prove if there is a statistically significant difference, if p

384
00:31:16,040 --> 00:31:21,440
 is less than 0.05, or if the difference, if any, is just due to chance, p is greater

385
00:31:21,440 --> 00:31:23,200
 than 0.05.

386
00:31:23,200 --> 00:31:27,940
 So hopefully, you can appreciate that, again, a lot of these concepts overlap and flow into

387
00:31:27,940 --> 00:31:32,960
 one another, which is great for us because it's less we have to memorize.

388
00:31:32,960 --> 00:31:38,920
 The z-test serves the same purpose as the t-test, but it's used if there's a larger

389
00:31:38,920 --> 00:31:46,400
 sample size, so n is greater than 30 is kind of a rough ballpark to use.

390
00:31:46,400 --> 00:31:51,640
 And if the standard deviation and variance are known.

391
00:31:51,640 --> 00:31:53,800
 So the z-test is a little bit better.

392
00:31:53,800 --> 00:31:58,560
 There are a couple other things between these two, but for the board exam, this is plenty

393
00:31:58,560 --> 00:32:00,560
 to know.

394
00:32:00,560 --> 00:32:06,320
 And lastly, we have ANOVA, analysis of variance, that's what it stands for.

395
00:32:06,320 --> 00:32:14,640
 And this is basically a t-test, but between two or more means.

396
00:32:14,640 --> 00:32:17,040
 All right, operational variables.

397
00:32:17,040 --> 00:32:20,160
 So these are important to define and know what they mean.

398
00:32:20,160 --> 00:32:25,640
 And again, lucky for us, it's really not too complicated once we boil all this down.

399
00:32:25,640 --> 00:32:32,080
 So qualitative means descriptive, these are qualities.

400
00:32:32,080 --> 00:32:41,080
 If we talk about nominal qualitative variables, nominal means they're based on names or labels.

401
00:32:41,080 --> 00:32:48,760
 So we could have binary variables like yes or no, right or left, or ternary variables,

402
00:32:48,760 --> 00:32:55,800
 which have three different options, and examples of nominal variables used in dental literature

403
00:32:55,800 --> 00:33:03,560
 could be genotype, blood type, hair color, eye color, eye color, et cetera.

404
00:33:03,560 --> 00:33:09,000
 And so these are things that you would use or see in like a chi-square test, these qualitative

405
00:33:09,000 --> 00:33:12,240
 descriptive variables.

406
00:33:12,240 --> 00:33:16,560
 Ordinal variables involve a ranking of some sort.

407
00:33:16,560 --> 00:33:23,800
 So examples of ordinal variables would include socioeconomic status, where you have low, middle,

408
00:33:23,800 --> 00:33:32,000
 or high income, education level, where you could have high school, bachelors, DDS, DMD,

409
00:33:32,000 --> 00:33:37,560
 or something, let's say, orthodontics related, like mild, moderate, or severe crowding.

410
00:33:37,560 --> 00:33:42,120
 So there's a clear ranking involved with the variables being used.

411
00:33:42,120 --> 00:33:48,640
 Now I put an asterisk here, because ordinal can also be quantitative.

412
00:33:48,640 --> 00:33:52,120
 So quantitative variables are numbers.

413
00:33:52,120 --> 00:33:53,120
 They're quantities.

414
00:33:53,120 --> 00:34:01,840
 So an example of an ordinal quantitative variable are things like the Likert scale, which is

415
00:34:01,840 --> 00:34:09,800
 you would ask on a survey in a cross-sectional study, rank this on a scale from one to seven,

416
00:34:09,800 --> 00:34:14,960
 with one being not at all likely, and seven being extremely likely.

417
00:34:14,960 --> 00:34:22,000
 Or a net promoter score, which you can see sometimes on surveys following the use of

418
00:34:22,000 --> 00:34:23,760
 some kind of product.

419
00:34:23,760 --> 00:34:29,200
 And so a net promoter score would rank from zero to ten, how likely you are to tell someone

420
00:34:29,200 --> 00:34:31,280
 about this product.

421
00:34:31,280 --> 00:34:36,520
 So these are rankings, but they're represented by numbers.

422
00:34:36,520 --> 00:34:42,240
 So and that can be really useful for statistics to have a clear quantitative values that you're

423
00:34:42,240 --> 00:34:44,680
 gathering.

424
00:34:44,680 --> 00:34:49,100
 Interval variables are a range of values.

425
00:34:49,100 --> 00:34:57,800
 So examples of interval variables include temperature, pH scale, SAT scores, et cetera.

426
00:34:57,800 --> 00:35:04,120
 And lastly, we have ratio variables, which involve a range of values again, but with

427
00:35:04,120 --> 00:35:07,800
 a clear definition of zero.

428
00:35:07,800 --> 00:35:15,680
 So examples of ratio variables include reaction rate, pulse, weight, length, temperature in

429
00:35:15,680 --> 00:35:22,800
 Kelvin, where we have an absolute zero, basically an interval variable, but with a clear zero

430
00:35:22,800 --> 00:35:25,000
 point.

431
00:35:25,000 --> 00:35:30,360
 So for qualitative variables, we're using the chi-square test.

432
00:35:30,360 --> 00:35:40,840
 For quantitative variables, we would be using a t-test, z-test, ANOVA, et cetera.

433
00:35:40,840 --> 00:35:43,280
 Some more definitions to know.

434
00:35:43,280 --> 00:35:45,960
 Independent variable, I've already thrown this around a little bit.

435
00:35:45,960 --> 00:35:49,520
 This is like our X, if we have an XY plot.

436
00:35:49,520 --> 00:35:58,000
 And so the conditions or factors of an experiment that are manipulated or controlled by the investigator.

437
00:35:58,000 --> 00:36:04,160
 So this would be like the type of treatment being compared, the placebo and the experimental

438
00:36:04,160 --> 00:36:06,920
 drug, things like that.

439
00:36:06,920 --> 00:36:13,400
 Whereas a dependent variable, or the Y, the Y axis, is the measure or outcome thought

440
00:36:13,400 --> 00:36:20,920
 to change as a result of the presence, absence, or manipulation of the independent variable.

441
00:36:20,920 --> 00:36:28,320
 So this would be like mortality, disease outcome, health status, cost, et cetera.

442
00:36:28,320 --> 00:36:32,840
 And lastly, we have the confounding variables or covariates.

443
00:36:32,840 --> 00:36:41,240
 So these, I mentioned before, can adversely affect the relationship between these two.

444
00:36:41,240 --> 00:36:46,280
 It muddies the waters, so to speak, between X and Y. And this can cause the researcher

445
00:36:46,280 --> 00:36:52,280
 to analyze the result incorrectly, the results may show a false correlation between X and

446
00:36:52,280 --> 00:36:58,000
 Y lead to an incorrect rejection of the null hypothesis, et cetera, that cause a lot of

447
00:36:58,000 --> 00:36:59,440
 different issues.

448
00:36:59,440 --> 00:37:06,280
 And confounders could be gender, age, ethnicity, anything that maybe wasn't controlled for

449
00:37:06,280 --> 00:37:08,800
 in the experiment.

450
00:37:08,800 --> 00:37:10,280
 So what do we do about this?

451
00:37:10,280 --> 00:37:15,640
 Well, we can minimize confounding variables by randomizing.

452
00:37:15,640 --> 00:37:22,440
 Because if we have large sample sizes with randomly sampled and allocated groups, hopefully

453
00:37:22,440 --> 00:37:28,720
 all of the confounding variables will be randomly and uniformly distributed among those different

454
00:37:28,720 --> 00:37:29,720
 groups.

455
00:37:29,720 --> 00:37:34,360
 So randomization is our best friend.

456
00:37:34,360 --> 00:37:39,560
 And lastly, we have the components of a scientific paper.

457
00:37:39,560 --> 00:37:45,680
 The PubMed is a common database used by medical and dental students, and medical and dental

458
00:37:45,680 --> 00:37:52,680
 professionals that contains a whole host of electronic dental literature.

459
00:37:52,680 --> 00:37:58,880
 So the board examiners like you to know the framework, the basic framework of a dental

460
00:37:58,880 --> 00:38:04,920
 paper or any scientific paper for that matter.

461
00:38:04,920 --> 00:38:09,640
 So the title is simply that, the title of the study which should indicate some central

462
00:38:09,640 --> 00:38:14,720
 question or focus of that particular journal article.

463
00:38:14,720 --> 00:38:19,560
 The abstract is essentially the SparkNotes version that appears at the beginning of the

464
00:38:19,560 --> 00:38:26,960
 paper and provides a quick summary of the background, the population being tested, findings, and

465
00:38:26,960 --> 00:38:29,400
 main conclusions.

466
00:38:29,400 --> 00:38:35,520
 The introduction section provides a background on the topic, why it's important, why it's

467
00:38:35,520 --> 00:38:41,280
 relevant, a literature review of what's currently available on the topic, and then clearly states

468
00:38:41,280 --> 00:38:46,400
 the purpose and aims of the study and a hypothesis for the study.

469
00:38:46,400 --> 00:38:52,320
 So you're no hypothesis while you're trying to disprove and ultimately what you're trying

470
00:38:52,320 --> 00:38:55,440
 to prove with your research.

471
00:38:55,440 --> 00:39:02,920
 The method section provides specific information about the population being tested, the independent

472
00:39:02,920 --> 00:39:09,360
 and dependent variables, the sampling method, the experimental design, the statistical

473
00:39:09,360 --> 00:39:14,240
 analyses performed, everything we've essentially covered in this video.

474
00:39:14,240 --> 00:39:20,080
 And a good litmus test is that the method section has enough information that anyone

475
00:39:20,080 --> 00:39:25,240
 would be able to replicate that study if they have the proper resources.

476
00:39:25,240 --> 00:39:31,400
 So the method section allows the reader to assess the validity and the reliability, right,

477
00:39:31,400 --> 00:39:34,560
 the quality of the diagnostic test being used.

478
00:39:34,560 --> 00:39:36,680
 And I think it's often overlooked.

479
00:39:36,680 --> 00:39:42,160
 This is probably arguably one of the most important sections of the paper.

480
00:39:42,160 --> 00:39:45,520
 And unfortunately, if someone's short on time, this is typically the section that people

481
00:39:45,520 --> 00:39:47,120
 will skim through.

482
00:39:47,120 --> 00:39:53,120
 But again, the method section tells you a lot of information about the paper and the research

483
00:39:53,120 --> 00:39:55,680
 that was conducted.

484
00:39:55,680 --> 00:40:00,500
 The results section describes specific findings and outcomes from the study.

485
00:40:00,500 --> 00:40:05,600
 The discussion section interprets and makes sense of those findings.

486
00:40:05,600 --> 00:40:11,960
 And the conclusion provides a summary and attempts to draw connections between the findings

487
00:40:11,960 --> 00:40:18,320
 and the original question and hypothesis that they introduced in the introduction section.

488
00:40:18,320 --> 00:40:21,440
 Or at least, the paper should do that.

489
00:40:21,440 --> 00:40:26,640
 And finally, the references at the end is a list of papers the authors used.

490
00:40:26,640 --> 00:40:31,600
 So people could go find additional resources if they wanted to learn more about a certain

491
00:40:31,600 --> 00:40:34,760
 aspect of the topic.

492
00:40:34,760 --> 00:40:38,480
 All right, so that's it for this video.

